{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "### Book Information\n\n**Title:** The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies  \n**Author:** Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri  \n**Year of Publication:** 2019  \n**Publisher:** Oceanic University Press  \n**ISBN:** 978-1-83748-291-5  \n**Summary:**  \nThis scholarly monograph, spanning 284 pages, challenges the perception of silence as merely the absence of speech, presenting it as a complex form of communication. Drawing on twelve years of ethnographic fieldwork across various Polynesian, Micronesian, and Melanesian communities, Dr. Tāwhiri develops a six-category typology of communicative silence. This includes concepts such as \"nooanoa\" (held relational silence) and \"faka'apa'apa\" (respectful deferential silence). The typical forms of silence are shown to be intertwined with kinship obligations, rituals, and daily social interactions. The introductory chapter features a detailed vignette from Fakarava atoll, reinforcing the argument that silence needs to be analyzed in its own right rather than as a mere byproduct of speech.\n\n**Key Themes:**\n- Non-verbal communication\n- Communicative silence\n- Pacific anthropology\n- Ethnolinguistics\n- Kinship and ritual\n- Indigenous epistemology\n- Typology of silence\n\n### Author Information\n\n**Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri** is an Associate Professor of Linguistic Anthropology at the University of Auckland and an affiliate researcher at the Australian National University. Her research centers on non-verbal and silence-based communication systems within Oceanic cultures, and she earned her PhD from ANU in 2006 under the guidance of Professor David Holton. Before publishing *The Deafening Silence*, she contributed 14 peer-reviewed articles to various esteemed journals such as *Language in Society*, *American Ethnologist*, and *Pacific Studies*.\n\nFor more detailed information, you can check her profiles:\n- [Faculty Profile, University of Auckland](https://openbooks.net/authors/mereana-tawhiri)\n- [Pacific Anthropology Network Profile](https://openbooks.net/scholars/mereana-tawhiri) \n\nThis contextualizes her expertise and contributions within the field of anthropology.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
